<PolicyLayout title="Service terms" description="The publishing service, subscription allowances and lifecycle for BlogStack accounts.">
 <h2>Your content and access</h2><p>You own your content and give BlogStack permission to store, process and deliver it to provide the service. You are responsible for content you publish, the rights to uploaded material and access you grant to team members. Keep authentication credentials and private API keys secure.</p>
 <h2>Subscriptions and allowances</h2><p>Plans are billed per account in USD. The selected recurring price, interval and applicable taxes appear at checkout. Blog, people, storage, external-check and AI allowances are shared across the account as shown on the pricing page. There are no automatic overage charges. Each blog supports up to 1,000 posts and each image up to 10 MiB.</p><p>The no-card Studio trial lasts 14 days from the first owned blog. Annual plans refresh AI and link-check allowances monthly. Paid access begins after a confirmed payment. Only the account owner can manage checkout and subscription changes.</p>
 <h2>Changes, cancellation and expiry</h2><p>Same-interval upgrades invoice a prorated difference and take effect after payment. Downgrades and interval changes take effect at renewal after usage fits the destination plan. Cancel through the dashboard payment portal; access continues through confirmed paid coverage.</p><p>Editing stops at expiry. Public delivery continues for 30 days, and content/media exports remain available until deletion at 90 days after advance warnings. A full refund or provider revocation may shorten paid coverage. Previously downloaded or customer-built content cannot be withdrawn through a BlogStack suspension.</p>
 <h2>AI and search</h2><p>AI suggestions require human review and save accepted edits as private drafts. Review accuracy and rights before publishing. SEO findings identify common issues and do not guarantee indexing, rankings or traffic. Visitor analytics may be sampled and are labeled as estimates where appropriate.</p>
 <h2>Integrations and open source</h2><p>Comments and newsletters use customer-supplied integrations. Their provider accounts, policies and charges remain separate. Downloaded public UI source remains available under its MIT license. Removing a paid branding setting does not revoke the license to source already obtained.</p>
 <h2>Acceptable use and support</h2><p>Do not use the service to publish unlawful material, distribute malware, abuse other users or bypass access controls and service allowances. Contact support about incidents, export requests and billing questions. See the refund request process for disputed or mistaken charges. Applicable statutory rights remain unaffected.</p>
</PolicyLayout>
